  "description": "A quick, practical guide to Synaptic Plasticity: Learning at the Synapse.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Every time you learn something new, a synapse somewhere in your brain gets physically stronger.",
      "visual": "💪"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Long-term potentiation (LTP) strengthens synapses that fire repeatedly—the basis of memory formation.",
      "visual": "🔗"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "The receiving neuron inserts more receptors into its membrane, making it more sensitive to signals.",
      "visual": "📡"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "The reverse also happens: long-term depression (LTD) weakens rarely used synapses.",
      "visual": "📉"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "This two-way tuning lets the brain strengthen important paths and prune useless ones continuously.",
      "visual": "✂️"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Learning isn't abstract. It's synapses getting louder or quieter.",
      "visual": "🔊"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What is long-term potentiation (LTP)?",
    "options": [
      "Strengthening of repeatedly activated synapses",
      "Death of inactive neurons",
      "Growth of new brain regions"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
